  write('backend-deletion-plan.txt',`${deletionActive ? 'IMPLEMENTED, DEPLOYED AND ENABLED / PERMANENT RUNTIME PERMISSIONS VERIFIED' : 'IMPLEMENTED AND DEPLOYED / GENERAL ACTIVATION DISABLED'} — final signed-device, retention and provider verification remain submission gates. Deployed 5 November: API, worker, direct lock connection and both additive migrations. The fresh backup passed an isolated PostgreSQL18 restore. See deployment/DEPLOYED-RELEASE-2026-11-05.txt and the latest canary result in deployment/index.html. See backend-account-deletion-runbook.txt for the current implementation, limits and rollout sequence.\nExisting checkout: ../testimonial-backend; no new clone needed.\nNEW LOCAL PREPARATION (5 NOVEMBER): dedicated direct Neon-compatible lock/migration connection, default-disabled acceptance/cleanup, canary allowlist and 81 passing backend tests. Migration/backfill/idempotency and separate backup restore passed with disposable local data. See deployment/DEPLOYMENT-ROLLBACK-RUNBOOK.txt, deployment/permanent-s3-cleanup-policy.json and evidence/backend-rollout-preparation.json. Permanent policy NOT attached. The later executed release completed direct runtime configuration, migrations and isolated backup restore; production activation and recovery/retention settings remain open.\nImplementation and future deployment checklist:\n1. Require authenticated account ownership and explicit destructive confirmation/re-authentication appropriate to the account type. The client never selects another distributor/client for deletion.\n2. Persist an idempotent deletion request. Immediately block new account access/uploads/confirmations; stateless JWTs must check deletion/account existence. A repeated request/retry must not re-enable the account.\n3. Stop/quiesce and cancel transcription/render jobs. Synchronize account deletion with in-flight workers so they cannot recreate transcript, analysis, objects or output after purge. Test races.\n4. Revoke/expire outstanding media capabilities; signed upload links already issued can outlive a request. Use a defined drain period/final sweep and handle multipart uploads.\n5. Enumerate the complete account-owned S3 prefix, including unconfirmed/orphaned originals, captions, derived/rendered objects and object versions/delete markers where versioning is enabled. Check IAM permission for listing/deleting; do not claim S3 version purge without it.\n6. Transactionally remove dependent transcript, analysis, rendered-video and segment/profile/account rows; remove or redact personal data in job payloads/errors as appropriate.\n7. Define lawful exceptions, log/backup expiry and provider-side deletion responsibilities; a local/S3 purge does not certify ElevenLabs, Gemini, backups or exported copies erased. Never promise everything instantly.\n8. App: Profile > Delete account, clear warning, explicit confirmation, progress/error/retry; after server acceptance wipe only that account's recordings/caches/consent/token, cancel active uploads and sign out. Preserve unrelated accounts' unsent files. Deleting a local take/logout is not this feature.\n9. Public deletion URL: functioning request/instructions page with data scope, verification, timing and retained-data details. A mailto draft alone is not completion of the in-app/backend requirement.\n10. Test auth/ownership, stale tokens, repeated requests, S3 pagination/versions, permission failures, partial retries, signed-link late PUT, in-flight jobs and worker restarts. Stage migrations and test using disposable accounts/objects. Deploy API/worker/schema together, then test the app. No production purge is authorized by this planning document.\n`);
